course: adult-life
lesson: 61
title: Olympiad: what is really going on ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson mixes the whole course into puzzles about reading grown-up life: what the words really mean, what the clues add up to, and who is the right person to ask.
explain: Grown-ups often say more or less than they mean. «I've told you a million times» is not a number to count, and «that's not ideal» after the boiler breaks in January can mean a real headache.
explain: Clues work like a detective's: one clue alone proves very little, but several clues pointing the same way make a good guess. A guess is still not a fact until someone checks it.
explain: Scene: in a kitchen in Leeds, Mum whispers on the phone, a big box is hidden on top of the wardrobe, and your birthday is on Saturday. Three clues, one happy answer: a surprise is being planned, and it is kinder not to go looking for it.
explain: For every puzzle ask three questions: What do the words really mean? What do all the clues say together? Who should I tell or ask?
fact: Hyperbole makes things sound bigger than they are («a million times», «a mile-long queue»), so its numbers are never the ones to count; understatement makes things sound smaller («a bit nippy», «not ideal», «a bit of a pickle», «a bit under the weather» = a little poorly).
fact: «I'm fine, it's just a scratch» about a real injury is the dangerous kind of downplaying: the wise move is to check it and tell an adult.
fact: One clue alone proves little; several clues pointing the same way make a good guess, and a guess still needs checking before it is treated as a fact.
fact: A story passed along a street often grows at each step, so the first, plainest version is usually closest to what really happened.
fact: The right helper depends on the problem: 999 for an emergency, the GP for an illness that can wait, the pharmacist for medicine advice, a teacher for a worry at school, a trusted grown-up for anything that frightens you.
fact: A closed door and low voices usually mean grown-up business, such as a worry, a plan or a surprise, and it is usually not about the child.
fact: When parents separate it is never the child's fault, and it cannot be undone by a child being extra good.
fact: «Sources close to» someone are people a newspaper does not name, so their claims cannot be checked by the reader.
fact: Signs of a scam: it is urgent, it asks for money, a PIN or a password, and it offers a prize or refund you never expected.
fact: In UK cinemas a 12A film may be seen by under-12s only with an adult; a 15 film may not be seen by anyone under 15, with or without an adult.
fact: «We can't afford it» can mean there is no money for it, or that the money is needed for something that matters more.
fact: A good grown-up who snaps when tired comes back later, says sorry, and makes clear it was not the child's fault.
fact: The last thing a speaker says after «no wait» or «sorry, I mean» is the one that counts.
exercise: Nan: «I've told you a million times, shoes off! That's twice today already.» How many times today, really? | 2 | 1 million | 1 | 3
exercise: Mum whispers on the phone, a big box is hidden on the wardrobe, your birthday is Saturday. What is most likely? | A birthday surprise is being planned | Mum is in big trouble with the bank | The family is moving house this Saturday | Mum is selling your old toys online
exercise: Which of these is NOT a sign of a scam? | A trip letter your teacher hands out in class | «Act now or your account will close» | «Send your PIN to claim your prize» | «You've won a draw you never entered»
exercise: Monday: «A van was outside Mr Hill's.» Tuesday: «Mr Hill's moving.» Wednesday: «Mr Hill's been kicked out!» What is actually known? | Only that a van was outside his house | That Mr Hill has been kicked out of his home | That Mr Hill is moving away this week | That Mr Hill could not pay his rent
exercise: Snow is falling sideways and Grandad says «Bit nippy out.» What does he really mean? | It is very cold | It is a little bit chilly | It is warm for winter | It is about to get sunny
exercise: Your mate falls off a wall, can't stand on his foot and says «it's nothing, I'm fine». What is the best first move? | Get a grown-up straight away | Believe him and carry on playing | Help him hop all the way home | Look it up on your phone first
exercise: Who is the best person to ask about which cough medicine Gran can take with her other tablets? | The pharmacist at the local chemist | The newsagent who sells sweets | A friend at school who had a cough | The bus driver who takes Gran to town
exercise: Gran has fallen, can't get up and is not making sense when she talks. Who do you call? | 999 | The GP surgery in the morning | The chemist to ask about plasters | Nobody until Mum gets home from work
exercise: A UK cinema shows a 12A film. Ava, 10, goes alone; Ben, 10, goes with his dad; Cal, 13, goes alone. Who cannot get in? | Ava | Ben | Cal | Ava and Ben
exercise: A cinema shows a 15 film. Which child is allowed in? | Mo, who is 15 today | Kim, 14, with her mum | Jo, 12, with her big sister, 18 | Raj, 14 and a half
exercise: A paper says: «Sources close to the singer say she is FURIOUS.» How much can a reader check? | Nothing, as the sources have no names | All of it, because it is in a paper | Some of it, by phoning the sources | All of it, the word is in capitals
exercise: Your friend says: «My parents split up because I was naughty.» Which reply is right? | It is never the child's fault, it's grown-up reasons | Yes, so be extra good and they'll get back together | Yes, children are the main reason parents split | No, it was the fault of the neighbours
exercise: Dad says «can't afford it» about a £2 comic, then buys Gran a £20 birthday present the same day. Which fits best? | The money went to what mattered more this month | Dad lied, he has lots of spare money | Comics cost more than presents do | Gran will pay him back twice over
exercise: Dad snaps at tea, then later says: «Sorry, I was shattered, it wasn't you.» What does this show? | A tired grown-up owning up to a mistake | That you did something very wrong at tea | That Dad will never snap ever again | That saying sorry is only for children
exercise: Mum: «The boiler's gone and the car's making that noise again. Not ideal.» How big is the problem? | A real one: two costly repairs at the same time | Tiny, she is only a little bit cross | None, she is joking about the noise | A disaster that means the family must move out
exercise: Odd one out: which grown-up line is NOT downplaying? | «I'm absolutely starving, I could eat a horse» | «It's a bit of a pickle» | «Not bad at all» about a brilliant goal | «Just a scratch» about a deep cut
test: Lily from Glasgow: «Ma says the queue at the post office was a mile long, nae kidding. How long was it, like?» What's the sensible reading? | A long queue, but nowhere near a mile | Exactly one mile of people | About 1,000 people in a line | Short, as she was being sarcastic about it
test: Jayden: «No cap, Mrs Patel's always rude, she shouted at me once in Year 2.» What's wrong with his claim? | One time years ago is not «always» | Nothing, once is enough to prove always | Mrs Patel was being kind when she shouted | He should have said «sometimes rude» instead
test: Kitchen door shuts, low voices, then Mum comes out with red eyes and says «Gran's in hospital, we'll know more tomorrow.» What should you do now? | Ask if you can help and wait for news | Listen at the door to find out more | Ring Gran's hospital ward yourself straight away | Tell your class Gran is very ill
test: Bruv in the group chat: «Free 1000 coins!! jus send ur login, ends in 5 mins!!» How many scam signs are in it? | Three: a prize, a rush, a login | One: the spelling is wrong | None, it ends soon so it's real | Two: the coins and the spelling
test: Overheard on the bus: «Our Jade's moving to Spain — no wait, sorry, to Spalding, near Boston.» Where is Jade moving? | Spalding | Spain | Boston | Near Spain
test: Your cousin, whose mum and dad split up last year, says «if I'm proper good they'll get back together.» What's the kind, true reply? | That's not how it works, and it was never your fault | Yes, if you try very hard they will | No, because your parents don't love you | Only if you also do all the chores
test: Gran from Cardiff, tamping: «That parcel text wants £1.99 by tonight or they send it back, the cheek!» What's going on? | A scam using a rush to get her card details | A real delivery firm being rude to her | A free gift that she must pay for quickly | The post office has lost her parcel by mistake
test: Your mate: «Your dad told me he's 'a bit under the weather'. Is he dying or what?» What does the phrase mean? | He feels a bit poorly, like a cold | He is very seriously ill in hospital | He is soaked from standing in the rain | He is sad about the weather forecast
test: Headline: «Prince in SHOCK snub!» The story says he «appeared not to wave» at one person. What do we really know? | In one photo he seems not to wave | He hates that person and said so | He refused to meet the person ever again | He was rude on purpose to the whole crowd
test: Mum, on nights all week, says «I'm grand, love» but nods off mid-sentence twice. What's really going on? | She is very tired from her shifts | She is completely fine and wide awake | She is bored of talking to you | She is pretending so you'll go out
test: A street story grew in three steps. Put them in order, first to last: A «Number 4 bought a giant telly, must've won the lottery!» B «Number 4 got a big parcel» C «Number 4 got a new telly» | B, C, A | A, C, B | C, B, A | B, A, C
exam: Determine which reading of the utterance «I have told you a million times» is accurate. | An exaggeration meaning «many times», not a count | A precise count of one million instances | A count of one thousand instances | An understatement of a larger figure
exam: Identify the person most appropriately consulted regarding whether a cough medicine may be taken alongside existing tablets. | A pharmacist | A newsagent | A school friend | A bus driver
exam: A rumour grows from «a van was outside» to «he was evicted». Select the version most likely closest to the truth. | The first and plainest version: a van was outside | The latest version, because it has been repeated most | The middle version, as an average of the others | Whichever version was told by the most people
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning children whose parents separate. | The separation is never the child's fault | The child's behaviour is usually the cause | Good behaviour can reverse a separation | Children must choose a parent at once
